# The Tale of the River and the Rain

In the beginning, the River flowed endlessly to the sea, giving all her waters without hope of return. The Rain watched from the clouds above, witnessing the River's generosity but holding back her own gift.

"Why should I fall?" thought the Rain. "The River gives freely to the ocean, who swallows everything and returns nothing. If I descend, I too will be consumed."

Years passed. The River grew shallow, her banks cracking like broken promises. The fish perished. The trees withered. Still the Rain withheld, fearing the loss of herself.

One desperate summer, the River called upward: "I have given all I have. The world drinks from me, but I am emptying. I cannot continue alone."

The Rain finally understood. "The River gives not from abundance, but from faith—faith that what is given will return."

That night, the Rain released herself, falling into the River's depleted bed. The River swelled with joy, flowing once more to the sea. And the sea, warmed by the sun, sent the Rain skyward again.

Thus began the eternal cycle: giving, receiving, returning. Each trusting the other to complete the sacred circle, knowing that withholding breaks the world, while trust renews it.
